After setting up a Conda environment to perform DVC in or switching to one where you intend to work on DVC you can install DVC with:
pip install dvc

A remote refers to a storage system for the data. (In our case it will likely either be a local file directory or the GPSC)

Add a remote: dvc remote add -d (makes default) <name> <path to another file, ssh, or other source for remote storage of hashed files>

Add a data source from outside the project: dvc import-url <flags*> <url or path to external directory>
* --no-download is a good flag to use as it prevents downloading the entire dataset immediately if it is a large dataset that you are working with
** This is separate from a remote, and this information about the data will be stored in the remote

Upload dataset or changes to dataset: dvc push
* This should be followed by a fresh git commit+ add whenever this happens to track the changes that were made to the dataset

Retrieve changes to the dataset from a local remote: dvc pull <file*>
* The file is an optional tag if you only want to pull the data for a specific dataset from its respective .dvc file

Retrieve changes to a dataset from a non-local remote: dvc update <file>
* This one is required if the dataset isn't being stored inside of the repo, which is true for our case

Remove a remote: dvc remote remove <name> 


